WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.720
As 2021 dawned, nearly every industry on&nbsp;
Earth was still suffering the effects of 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.960
including the world of television. Here are&nbsp;
some of the familiar favorites leaving the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:15.920
airwaves in 2021, and the&nbsp;
often strange reasons why.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.360
“Peaky Blinders” is one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:23.280
of the most ambitious crime dramas to ever grace&nbsp;
television. Based somewhat on the true story of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:28.880
a street gang that terrorized Birmingham and other&nbsp;
parts of the U.K. in the years between World War I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:35.680
and World War II, the dark, violent, and stylish&nbsp;
show evoked underworld sagas like “The Godfather”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:41.520
It also presented a view of the interwar&nbsp;
period rarely approached in media, along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:47.280
with showcasing an all-star cast that included&nbsp;
Cillian Murphy, Tom Hardy, and Anya Taylor-Joy.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:53.520
“Peaky Blinders” remained a popular,&nbsp;
much-discussed series well into its run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:59.520
both on the BBC in the U.K., and&nbsp;
on Netflix in the U.S. In May 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:04.160
creator Steven Knight told the “Birmingham&nbsp;
Press Club” just before the debut of season five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:08.800
that a sixth season of “Peaky Blinders” was&nbsp;
on the way, with a seventh season likely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:14.880
if not probable. However, after pandemic-related&nbsp;
shutdowns delayed the shooting of season six,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.680
Knight changed his plans. He told “Variety,”

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:22.080
"COVID came along, and we lost a year of&nbsp;
production. So we put our heads together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.640
and thought that it would be a good idea to&nbsp;
do a movie instead of doing series seven."

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:30.534
That means “Peaky Blinders” fans&nbsp;
can at least look forward to that.

00:01:30.534 --> 00:01:34.240
In 2016, HBO debuted “Insecure,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:40.320
starring co-creator Issa Rae as Issa Dee, a woman&nbsp;
in her late twenties trying to balance all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:45.840
different threads in her life while searching for&nbsp;
fulfillment and purpose. When the series begins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:51.280
Issa works at a Los Angeles nonprofit designed&nbsp;
to help young students of color succeed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:57.200
Acclaimed for being a thoughtful and vital look&nbsp;
at 21st century life for African-American women,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.440
“Insecure” scored an Emmy nomination&nbsp;
for Outstanding Comedy Series.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:06.160
Insecure was a fully fleshed-out, grander&nbsp;
realization of “Awkward Black Girl,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:11.520
Rae's confessional YouTube series from the&nbsp;
2010s. Along with co-creator Larry Wilmore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:15.920
Rae mapped out a show designed to tell a&nbsp;
finite story about a certain period in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:20.000
life of her character and her social&nbsp;
circle. Rae shared with “Deadline,”

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.200
"We always planned to tell this&nbsp;
story through five seasons."

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:27.360
HBO renewed the series for&nbsp;
its fifth season in May 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.120
which Rae confirmed in January&nbsp;
2021 would be its last.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:38.160
Set in a police precinct deep in New York City,&nbsp;
“Brooklyn Nine-Nine” is one of the most acclaimed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:44.400
sitcoms of the 2010s and beyond. Revolving around&nbsp;
the goofy but actually talented detective Jake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:49.920
Peralta, who is played by Andy Samberg, the&nbsp;
crew of the “Nine-Nine” is more like a family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:56.000
It’s led by stone-faced captain Raymond Holt,&nbsp;
played by Andre Braugher, and involves neurotic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:02.080
sergeant Terry Jeffords, played by Terry Crews,&nbsp;
Jake-worshipping detective Boyle, played by Joe Lo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:08.480
Truglio, and self-absorbed community liaison Gina,&nbsp;
played by Chelsea Peretti. “Brooklyn Nine-Nine’s”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:14.960
pull was strong, seeing that when Fox canceled&nbsp;
it in 2018, it was immediately revived by NBC.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:15.920
“This isn’t over yet.”

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:23.200
“Brooklyn Nine-Nine's” eighth season was&nbsp;
delayed. Set to air during the 2019-2020 season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:28.960
coronavirus-related shutdowns of Hollywood&nbsp;
productions pushed back those episodes. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:33.360
after a spate of deaths of African-Americans&nbsp;
at the hands of law enforcement, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:39.040
those of Breonna Taylor and George Floyd, the&nbsp;
anti-police-brutality and overall anti-police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:44.320
sentiment that in part fueled coast-to-coast&nbsp;
protests led to a reckoning over cop shows.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:50.560
Shows thought to glorify police, like “Cops” and&nbsp;
“Live P.D.,” were immediately canceled. Producers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:56.080
for “Brooklyn Nine-Nine,” a gentle comedy about&nbsp;
police officers, tossed all the scripts written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:01.440
for its eighth season in order to start over&nbsp;
with recent events in mind. All of this weighed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:06.640
heavily on an aging show that was not a ratings&nbsp;
hit. NBC announced that “Brooklyn Nine-Nine”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:12.480
would return for its eighth and final season in&nbsp;
2021, with a limited run of just 10 episodes.

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:18.640
OWN's “The Haves and the Have Nots” is a&nbsp;
good old-fashioned 1980s-style, scandal-free,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:24.640
family drama-loaded primetime soap. Set in the&nbsp;
beautiful and moneyed city of Savannah, Georgia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:30.480
the story centers in part on the locally dominant&nbsp;
Cryers and Harringtons, who have abundant wealth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:35.600
and resources at their disposal. Those are the&nbsp;
"haves," while the "have nots" are the Youngs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:41.440
a working-class family led by single mother Hanna,&nbsp;
played by Crystal Fox, who works in the home of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:46.640
the Cryers. There's plenty of intrigue with the&nbsp;
servants, as well as when the two sides of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:52.320
socioeconomic coin interact, making for a show&nbsp;
that feels like a modern-day “Downton Abbey.”

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:57.040
The series comes from the mind of prolific&nbsp;
show creator and producer Tyler Perry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:02.400
In 2017, he signed a deal to make scripted&nbsp;
series for the channel. The first show under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:08.160
that deal was “The Haves and the Have Nots,” OWN's&nbsp;
first scripted series, which drew an average of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:12.960
three million viewers, which was huge for the&nbsp;
sometimes struggling network. After a run of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:19.360
eight years and nearly 200 episodes, “The Haves&nbsp;
and the Have Nots” will end its run in May 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:24.480
the last vestige of Perry's contract with the&nbsp;
Oprah Winfrey Network. He moved on from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:30.800
channel in 2017, signing a development deal to&nbsp;
make shows for Viacom outlets, including “BET.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:35.360
The end of this show marks a clean, contractual&nbsp;
break with Perry's former creative home.

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:40.400
“Mom” was a tricky sitcom from day one,&nbsp;
but the cast and crew pulled it off.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:45.200
Co-created by “Two and a Half Men” and “The&nbsp;
Big Bang Theory” mastermind Chuck Lorre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:50.960
“Mom” mined humor from addiction recovery.&nbsp;
“Scary Movie” star Anna Faris played Christy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:55.680
a recovering alcoholic in the early stages of&nbsp;
putting her life back together while balancing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:00.400
family responsibilities. She did all this while&nbsp;
dealing with the handful that was her mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:05.040
Bonnie, played by Allison Janney, also&nbsp;
dealing with her own addiction issues.

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:10.800
In September 2020, just days before “Mom” was&nbsp;
set to start production on its eighth season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:16.720
Faris abruptly walked away from the series. When&nbsp;
the show returned to the air two months later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:21.760
“Mom” writers got rid of Christy by explaining&nbsp;
that she'd moved away to attend law school at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:27.600
Georgetown University. Months later, CBS announced&nbsp;
that “Mom” would end at the conclusion of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:33.120
only season without Faris. The show arguably&nbsp;
couldn't suffer the blow of losing its star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:37.360
but Christy's exit also marked the&nbsp;
final step in the “Mom” story arc.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:43.280
When the show began in 2013, it was a vehicle for&nbsp;
Faris, and the action centered on her character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:48.480
a single mother raising two children. Over&nbsp;
time, the kids were completely written out and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:52.880
the spotlight moved over to Christy's mother&nbsp;
Bonnie, and her Alcoholics Anonymous group.

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:56.400
The “NCIS” format follows highly-trained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:02.320
Naval Criminal Investigative Service agents as&nbsp;
they investigate and solve Navy-related crimes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:08.480
The “NCIS” premise is one that keeps on giving.&nbsp;
Since 2003, the franchise has generated more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:16.240
than 400 episodes of the Washington, D.C. based&nbsp;
“NCIS,” nearly 300 of spinoff “NCIS: Los Angeles,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:21.840
and about 150 of “NCIS: New Orleans,”&nbsp;
starring veteran TV actor Scott Bakula&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:27.520
as team leader Supervisory Special Agent Dwayne&nbsp;
Cassius Pride. This iteration of the crime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:34.960
procedural saga hit CBS in 2014, and at one point,&nbsp;
ranked among the top 10 most watched shows on TV.

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:41.760
But that was years ago. In February 2021, CBS&nbsp;
announced that “NCIS: New Orleans” would end at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:46.160
the conclusion of its seventh season. The&nbsp;
cancellation comes in part due to falling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:52.960
viewership. In the 2020-21 season, no CBS drama&nbsp;
suffered a bigger drop than “NCIS: New Orleans,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.960 --> 00:08:00.560
which also loses 750,000 viewers from its lead-in&nbsp;
show, “NCIS: Los Angeles,” and accumulates a total&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:06.320
draw of about half of the original “NCIS.”&nbsp;
It especially didn't make sense for CBS to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:10.720
keep making new episodes of the New Orleans&nbsp;
series because it didn't generate big money in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:16.880
reruns and on streaming, the way the other two&nbsp;
NCIS shows do. Meanwhile, “NCIS: New Orleans”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:23.040
showrunners have already agreed to take over&nbsp;
a potential new CBS drama: “NCIS: Hawaii.”

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:27.440
With four distinct iterations airing decades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:33.280
apart, “The Twilight Zone” is one of the most&nbsp;
oft-revived shows in TV history. The original,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:41.840
hosted by creator Rod Serling, aired from 1959&nbsp;
to 1964, followed by a 1985 to 1989 version,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:50.160
another from 2002 to 2003, and a fourth, debuting&nbsp;
in 2019 on CBS All Access. Standing in for Serling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:55.920
was Academy Award-winning “Get Out” and “Us”&nbsp;
filmmaker Jordan Peele, who also co-developed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:01.280
the show. Like its predecessor, this modern-day&nbsp;
“Twilight Zone” told spooky, unsettling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:07.120
and hard-to-forget stories of fright, dread,&nbsp;
regret, and other dark parts of the human psyche.

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:12.560
Right around the time that ViacomCBS prepared&nbsp;
to convert its CBS All Access service into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:17.360
Paramount Plus, the producers in charge of this&nbsp;
latest "Twilight Zone" series announced that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:22.800
they would not be making new episodes for the&nbsp;
rebooted streamer. Peele and co-producer Simon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:26.960
Kinberg decided that they'd accomplished what&nbsp;
they'd set out to do. According to the “New York&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:31.760
Daily News” Peele and Kinberg's production&nbsp;
companies shared a joint statement, saying,

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:36.480
"After 20 unique episodes, we have told&nbsp;
the stories that we wanted to tell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:40.320
and CBS All Access was gracious in&nbsp;
their understanding of our decision."

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:43.520
“Wynnona Earp” is one of the wildest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:49.360
and most original TV series in recent memory.&nbsp;
The show depicts the adventures of the modern-day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:55.280
descendant of strong-armed Old West lawman Wyatt&nbsp;
Earp, who returns to her Rocky Mountains hometown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.280 --> 00:10:00.160
where she does battle with "revenants,” the&nbsp;
supernatural monster versions of the 19th century&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:03.600
scofflaws her great-great-grandfather&nbsp;
killed in the line of duty.

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:09.680
The show debuted in the U.S. on Spike in 2016,&nbsp;
and after three seasons moved to SyFy for season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:15.360
four. That network was supposed to air a fifth&nbsp;
season before reversing course in February 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:20.320
announcing that the fourth run would be the last&nbsp;
for "Wynnona Earp". It's a near miracle that those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:26.240
episodes were even finished. Production company&nbsp;
IDW ran out of money, which delayed filming for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:31.280
almost a year. SyFy agreed to foot the bill, but&nbsp;
then the pandemic led to a shutdown shortly after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:37.200
filming began. And then, SyFy's parent company,&nbsp;
NBCUniversal, restructured its programming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:42.160
strategy, deciding to emphasize streaming&nbsp;
content instead of cable network originals.

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:48.320
“Killing Eve” is a thrilling show — a darkly&nbsp;
comic, action-packed, high-stakes chase drama&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:54.080
about a brutal, globetrotting assassin named&nbsp;
Villanelle, played by Jodie Comer and Sandra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:59.360
Oh’s character Eve, the American-born British&nbsp;
intelligence officer in constant pursuit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:03.280
They're both obsessed with one another&nbsp;
for varied and complicated reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:07.440
and in its early seasons, “Killing Eve"&nbsp;
was one of the hottest shows on TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:12.960
in the U.K. and in the U.S., with Oh winning&nbsp;
a Golden Globe and Comer winning an Emmy.

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:17.920
The show has a very finite premise —&nbsp;
eventually, Eve and Villanelle have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:23.600
to come face to face for a final showdown,&nbsp;
with one winner and one loser — and in 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:29.040
AMC announced that the show would end after its&nbsp;
fourth season. Those episodes were delayed due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:34.560
to pandemic-related production shutdowns, and&nbsp;
the season will consist of only eight episodes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:39.280
which are set to air in 2022. However,&nbsp;
while “Killing Eve” is dead, the franchise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:44.960
will continue. In a statement, AMC original&nbsp;
programming president Dan McDermott shared,

00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:48.480
“[The network is] exploring potential&nbsp;
extensions of this compelling universe."

00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:51.360
So, hopefully, some kind of spinoff is on the way.

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:57.760
Celebrated and immensely popular fantasy author&nbsp;
Neil Gaiman published one of his masterworks in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.760 --> 00:12:04.320
2001, the novel “American Gods.” The story depicts&nbsp;
the ancient mythological and religious deities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:10.960
of Europe, Africa, and Asia brought to the United&nbsp;
States over centuries of immigration and diaspora,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:14.480
struggling to stay alive as their&nbsp;
adherents die out and also waging a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:20.080
battle with powerful "new gods" like Media&nbsp;
and the technology allegory Technical Boy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.080 --> 00:12:24.000
It's a complicated, heady brew, and&nbsp;
it was often held up as an example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:28.720
of an "unfilmable" work, a novel&nbsp;
impossible to reasonably adapt to film.

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:35.920
And yet, in 2017, Starz brought “American Gods”&nbsp;
to the small screen, in the form of a continuing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:41.680
weekly series in the hands of screenwriter Michael&nbsp;
Green and Bryan Fuller. However, “American Gods”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:47.760
experienced a lot of backstage issues. Toward&nbsp;
the end of the show's first season, showrunners&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:52.720
Green and Fuller departed, unable to resolve&nbsp;
creative differences with Starz executives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:57.360
and cast members Kristin Chenoweth and&nbsp;
Gillian Anderson followed them out the door.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:02.880
More showrunners came and went, actors reportedly&nbsp;
rewrote their own scripts to improve them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:07.520
and then cast member Orlando Jones was fired,&nbsp;
which he believes was racially motivated.

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:12.880
Starz ended the whole thing in March&nbsp;
2021, announcing that “American Gods'”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:16.640
already-aired third season — which ended&nbsp;
with a cliffhanger — would be the show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:21.600
last. The network added that a finale movie&nbsp;
or miniseries in the future was a possibility.

00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:26.880
Creator Emily Spivey, a writer for “Parks&nbsp;
and Recreation” and “King of the Hill,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:31.280
based “Bless the Harts” on elements and&nbsp;
characters from her North Carolina hometown.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:34.960
The animated family sitcom is&nbsp;
loaded with comedic heavy hitters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:40.000
with Kristen Wiig voicing single mother&nbsp;
and fish restaurant waitress Jenny Hart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:45.280
Maya Rudolph portraying Jenny's aggressive&nbsp;
and wild mother, Betty, Ike Barinholtz as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:50.480
Jenny's boyfriend, Wayne, and Jillian Bell&nbsp;
as Jenny's artsy teenage daughter, Violet.

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:55.600
A show full of crackling comedy and heart, “Bless&nbsp;
the Harts” fit right in on Fox's Sunday night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.600 --> 00:14:00.720
animation lineup alongside similar series like&nbsp;
“The Simpsons” and “Bob's Burgers.” However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:05.920
in the midst of the show's second season, Fox&nbsp;
announced that it would not order a third season.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.920 --> 00:14:12.560
The reason? Not just bad ratings but extremely&nbsp;
bad ratings. In season two, “Bless the Harts”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:18.880
pulled in only 1.3 million viewers on average,&nbsp;
making it Fox's lowest-rated Sunday night show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.880 --> 00:14:23.200
and second least-watched show&nbsp;
of the 2020-21 season overall.

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:28.560
In the midst of the great mid-2010s&nbsp;
reboot and revival craze that returned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:31.760
everything from “Twin Peaks” to&nbsp;
“Murphy Brown” to television,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:36.720
CBS ordered a new version of “MacGyver.”&nbsp;
The original show premiered in 1985,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:41.760
and this innovative action series focused on a&nbsp;
brilliant secret agent, played by Richard Dean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:46.400
Anderson, whose aversion to firearms meant that&nbsp;
he had to use his wits and whatever items he could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:51.840
find to save the day and beat the bad guys.&nbsp;
On the new “MacGyver,” which debuted in 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:57.360
Lucas Till played the role, only his version&nbsp;
was younger and more charming of a rogue.

00:14:57.360 --> 00:15:02.480
“MacGyver” was a top 30 hit in its first year,&nbsp;
but by its fifth — and ultimately final – season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:08.080
it ranked #10 in viewership of CBS'&nbsp;
14 dramas. Prior to the season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:14.160
CBS fired executive producer Peter M. Lenkov for&nbsp;
fostering a difficult work environment, bolstered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:19.600
by allegations from Till in “Vanity Fair” that&nbsp;
he'd been verbally abused and body-shamed on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:25.120
set to the degree that he'd felt suicidal. And&nbsp;
CBS was also involved in a “MacGyver” lawsuit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.120 --> 00:15:30.400
In December 2018, the rights holders of the&nbsp;
original “MacGyver” sued over unpaid royalties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.400 --> 00:15:34.640
generated by the new series. According to&nbsp;
“Deadline,” just before canceling the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.640 --> 00:15:39.840
CBS released a statement calling out the suit&nbsp;
for, quote, "fatal, incurable deficiencies."

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:45.440
If you or someone you know is having suicidal&nbsp;
thoughts, please call the National Suicide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.440 --> 00:15:55.360
Prevention Lifeline at 1-800-273-TALK (8255)&nbsp;
or text HOME to the Crisis Text Line at 741741.

00:15:57.280 --> 00:16:00.960
Plenty of comedians have hit in big in&nbsp;
their native United Kingdom and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:05.280
similarly found fame and fortune in&nbsp;
North America, like Sacha Baron Cohen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:10.080
Ricky Gervais, and the members of Monty&nbsp;
Python. Katherine Ryan did the opposite.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.080 --> 00:16:16.080
Born and raised in Canada, she became a popular&nbsp;
comic and panel show guest in England. In 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.080 --> 00:16:21.200
Netflix in the U.S. attempted to bring Ryan closer&nbsp;
to home, picking up "The Duchess," a six-episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:26.480
sitcom starring and created by the comedian. Ryan&nbsp;
portrayed a character also named Katherine, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.480 --> 00:16:31.840
single mother looking to expand her family if she&nbsp;
can find a man worthy enough of being the father.

00:16:31.840 --> 00:16:36.800
Overall, critics hailed "The Duchess," but Netflix&nbsp;
didn't think enough subscribers gave it a shot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.800 --> 00:16:41.520
On an episode of "The Secret To..."&nbsp;
podcast released in April 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:46.080
Ryan revealed that no more episodes of&nbsp;
"The Duchess" would be produced, saying,

00:16:46.080 --> 00:16:50.800
"I think something like 10 million people&nbsp;
watched it in 28 days, and that wasn't enough.”

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:56.720
There's also the issue of Ryan's schedule. A few&nbsp;
weeks before "The Duchess" news broke, Netflix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.720 --> 00:17:01.440
competitor Amazon signed her to make a new comedy&nbsp;
show called "Backstage with Katherine Ryan."

00:17:02.320 --> 00:17:08.080
It's a testament to the quality and universality&nbsp;
of Sherlock Holmes that more than 130 years after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.080 --> 00:17:12.400
the character created by Sir Arthur&nbsp;
Conan Doyle made his literary debut,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.400 --> 00:17:15.920
filmmakers and screenwriters are still&nbsp;
coming up with new and innovative ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.920 --> 00:17:20.240
to present and interpret the world of&nbsp;
the brilliant, deductive detective.

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:26.480
And in March 2021, the eight-episode first&nbsp;
season of "The Irregulars" hit Netflix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:29.680
detailing the adventures&nbsp;
of the scrappy, streetwise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.680 --> 00:17:35.280
19th-century London ragamuffins who act as helpers&nbsp;
and informants for Holmes. Out of the gate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:40.480
"The Irregulars" was a major hit, ranking at&nbsp;
one point as the most-watched show on streaming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.480 --> 00:17:48.160
with viewers watching 643 million minutes' worth&nbsp;
and spending 18 straight days in Netflix's top 10.

00:17:48.160 --> 00:17:53.040
Why Netflix decided against identifying "The&nbsp;
Irregulars" as a show it could renew is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:58.640
mystery only Sherlock Holmes himself could solve.&nbsp;
Not only was "The Irregulars" a bona fide smash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.640 --> 00:18:03.040
but the streaming service gave no&nbsp;
reason for its April 2021 decision.

00:18:04.240 --> 00:18:10.240
"The Last Kingdom" first hit TV screens in 2015&nbsp;
as a BBC series, and then Netflix brought the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.240 --> 00:18:15.440
sweeping historical drama — which occupied&nbsp;
a similar niche as shows like "Outlander"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.440 --> 00:18:21.600
and "Vikings" — to stateside audiences in 2018.&nbsp;
Based on "The Saxon Stories," the bestselling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:26.720
13-novel series by Bernard Cornwell, "The Last&nbsp;
Kingdom" is set in the British Isles in the late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.720 --> 00:18:33.200
800s, where Saxons have to contend with Vikings,&nbsp;
invading hordes, and power struggles. Alexander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.200 --> 00:18:38.880
Dreymon stars as Uhtred, a warrior deeply&nbsp;
conflicted about where his loyalties ought to lie.

00:18:38.880 --> 00:18:44.000
In April 2021, production company Carnival&nbsp;
Films, in conjunction with Netflix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.000 --> 00:18:47.840
announced that the fifth season of "The Last&nbsp;
Kingdom" — at the time, in the middle of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.840 --> 00:18:53.280
production — would be the series' last. According&nbsp;
to a statement released by executive producer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:58.160
Nigel Marchant, it sounds as if "The Last&nbsp;
Kingdom" has naturally reached the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.160 --> 00:19:03.440
or the near-end, of the story it set out to tell.&nbsp;
However, while the fifth season will reportedly be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.440 --> 00:19:08.480
based on the ninth and tenth books in Cornwell's&nbsp;
series, there are still two additional books that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:13.040
will remain untouched, which is a little&nbsp;
curious. Regardless, as Marchant put it,

00:19:13.040 --> 00:19:15.360
"We're excited to give viewers a chance to follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:19.680
Uhtred on the next stage of his epic&nbsp;
quest, where not everyone survives."

00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:25.920
"Prodigal Son" was a dark crime procedural,&nbsp;
but it offered a twist on the enduring format.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:30.480
A riff on "The Silence of the Lambs," the&nbsp;
series centered on New York Police Department&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.480 --> 00:19:35.920
criminal profiler Malcolm Bright, played by&nbsp;
Tom Payne, who got into the minds of suspects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.920 --> 00:19:40.880
by consulting a notorious, captured serial&nbsp;
killer played by Michael Sheen. "Prodigal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.880 --> 00:19:46.240
Son" was one part cat-and-mouse game, one part&nbsp;
unconventional father-son-reconnecting drama.

00:19:46.240 --> 00:19:52.000
In May 2021, “TVLine” reported that Fox had&nbsp;
decided against ordering a third season of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.000 --> 00:19:56.960
"Prodigal Son," meaning that it's already shot&nbsp;
and edited second-season finale would serve as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.960 --> 00:20:01.840
a series capper. According to a Fox insider,&nbsp;
executives really liked the show and wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.840 --> 00:20:06.960
to make it work, but its ratings were so low&nbsp;
that it wouldn't make economic sense to do so.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.960 --> 00:20:11.600
"Prodigal Son" viewership numbers fell&nbsp;
nearly 40 percent in its second season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:14.800
and it was the least-watched drama&nbsp;
still standing on the Fox schedule.

00:20:15.520 --> 00:20:18.320
With the breadth and ambition of a gigantic novel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:23.360
"This Is Us" has told both the big and&nbsp;
small stories in the lives of the Pearsons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.360 --> 00:20:28.480
often jumping forward or backward in time to&nbsp;
depict every major moment in the family's history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.480 --> 00:20:33.360
as well as the causes and aftermath. An&nbsp;
emotionally driven and tear-jerking show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:38.400
"This Is Us" has been a massive hit for NBC&nbsp;
and earned Emmy nominations and wins for most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:43.600
of its primary cast, including Sterling K.&nbsp;
Brown, Milo Ventimiglia, and Mandy Moore.

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:50.000
In May 2019, NBC gave "This Is Us" a three-season&nbsp;
renewal, ensuring the timeline-hopping family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:56.000
drama would stay on the air through its sixth year&nbsp;
and the 2021-22 season. That would turn out to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.000 --> 00:21:01.040
the last time NBC would order more episodes of&nbsp;
"This Is Us." When the network announced its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.040 --> 00:21:08.800
plans for 2021-22 in May 2021, it confirmed that&nbsp;
"This Is Us" would air its last episodes during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.800 --> 00:21:15.360
that time. This isn't really a true cancelation,&nbsp;
however — six seasons was part of the master plan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:15.360 --> 00:21:20.320
After year three, showrunner Isaac Aptaker&nbsp;
told “Deadline” that the series would end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.320 --> 00:21:26.640
in about three seasons in the future, and in 2020,&nbsp;
creator Dan Fogelman told the "Los Angeles Times",

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:30.640
"By the time we're in our sixth season,&nbsp;
you'll have a lot of the information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.640 --> 00:21:34.880
about what's happened to this family, and&nbsp;
what will be left is some resolution."

00:21:34.880 --> 00:21:36.320
“We’re all gonna be fine.”

00:21:37.200 --> 00:21:43.600
In 2003, amidst the traditionally combative,&nbsp;
confrontational, and personal problems-oriented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:43.600 --> 00:21:48.480
world of daytime talk programs, viewers were&nbsp;
treated to the "The Ellen DeGeneres Show."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:48.480 --> 00:21:53.040
The host, the dry and deadpan comedian&nbsp;
best known for the '90s sitcom "Ellen,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.040 --> 00:21:58.320
injected late-night style fun into her afternoon&nbsp;
show, engaging in light-hearted interviews with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.320 --> 00:22:03.760
celebrities, when she wasn't pranking them, as&nbsp;
well as recognizing and rewarding local heroes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:03.760 --> 00:22:09.120
and then dancing through every spare moment.&nbsp;
In May 2021, DeGeneres told “The Hollywood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.120 --> 00:22:13.680
Reporter” that she would shut down "The Ellen&nbsp;
DeGeneres Show" when her contract to host the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.680 --> 00:22:19.040
series ended in 2022. The main reason? It&nbsp;
no longer thrilled her. DeGeneres shared,

00:22:19.680 --> 00:22:23.200
"When you're a creative person, you&nbsp;
constantly need to be challenged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:23.200 --> 00:22:28.320
and as great as this show is, and as fun as&nbsp;
it is, it's just not a challenge anymore."

00:22:28.320 --> 00:22:34.800
DeGeneres may also be bailing on a sinking TV&nbsp;
ship. Ratings had fallen by nearly half from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:34.800 --> 00:22:40.240
2020 to 2021, in the wake of allegations&nbsp;
and revelations that "The Ellen DeGeneres&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.240 --> 00:22:43.840
Show" was a workplace fraught&nbsp;
with hostility and misbehavior.

00:22:44.800 --> 00:22:51.040
Over its first seven years, "Black-ish" fit in&nbsp;
nicely with ABC's line-up of domestic sitcoms like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:51.040 --> 00:22:56.080
"Modern Family" and "The Middle," but it was also&nbsp;
much more thoughtful and ambitious than the usual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.080 --> 00:23:02.160
exasperated-working-parents-with-rambunctious-kids&nbsp;
shows. Created by Kenya Barris, "Black-ish"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:02.160 --> 00:23:08.320
starred Anthony Anderson and Tracee Ellis Ross as&nbsp;
Dre and Bow Johnson, parents of four attempting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:08.320 --> 00:23:12.640
raise their children into well-rounded and&nbsp;
self-assured people while keeping wealthy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.640 --> 00:23:17.600
privilege in check and ensuring that they never&nbsp;
lose touch with their Black culture and identity.

00:23:17.600 --> 00:23:23.680
In May 2021, ABC renewed but also canceled&nbsp;
"Black-ish," announcing that the thoughtful family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.680 --> 00:23:30.480
sitcom would return sometime in the 2021-22 season&nbsp;
for a final stretch of episodes. "Black-ish" just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:30.480 --> 00:23:35.200
might've run its course. Initially a show about&nbsp;
raising kids, most of the young characters had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:35.200 --> 00:23:41.040
come of age. The eldest Johnson daughter, Zoey,&nbsp;
was already spun off into the Freeform series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.040 --> 00:23:47.360
"Grown-ish." Furthermore, "Black-ish" was one of&nbsp;
the least-watched shows on network TV in 2019-20&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:47.360 --> 00:23:54.240
season, and the ratings doldrums continued&nbsp;
in 2020-21, helping ABC make its decision.

00:23:54.880 --> 00:24:00.480
"Mixed-ish," an '80s-set spinoff about the&nbsp;
childhood of Tracee Ellis Ross' character, Bow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:00.480 --> 00:24:06.960
also didn't survive. ABC canceled the show after&nbsp;
two seasons in May 2021, effective immediately.

00:24:08.080 --> 00:24:13.360
"American Housewife" was something of a throwback&nbsp;
to sitcoms of the '80s and '90s, depicting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:13.360 --> 00:24:19.360
day-to-day lives of the argumentative and lightly&nbsp;
dysfunctional Otto family. Geeky dad Greg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:19.360 --> 00:24:25.200
played by Diedrich Bader, and mischievous mom&nbsp;
Katy, played by Katy Mixon, kept watch over their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:25.200 --> 00:24:31.120
three headstrong kids — dumb teen Taylor, played&nbsp;
by Meg Donnelly, money-obsessed Oliver, played by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:31.120 --> 00:24:37.520
Daniel DiMaggio, and idiosyncratic Anna-Kat,&nbsp;
played by Julia Butters — while trying to remain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:37.520 --> 00:24:43.120
the only scrupulous and tight-knit family in a&nbsp;
superficial and ultra-wealthy Connecticut town.

00:24:43.120 --> 00:24:47.680
Heading into its fifth season, "American&nbsp;
Housewife" faced personnel issues that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:47.680 --> 00:24:52.560
couldn't quite overcome. Julia Butters,&nbsp;
who played Anna-Kat, left the show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:52.560 --> 00:24:56.960
after a breakout performance in Quentin&nbsp;
Tarantino's "Once Upon a Time in Hollywood."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:56.960 --> 00:25:02.720
The role was recast and the character&nbsp;
rewritten with Giselle Eisenberg. Carly Hughes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:02.720 --> 00:25:08.080
who portrayed Katie Otto's friend and confidante&nbsp;
Angela, also departed, telling “Deadline,”

00:25:08.080 --> 00:25:10.320
“[I] was no longer able to work in the toxic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:10.320 --> 00:25:13.120
environment that was created&nbsp;
on 'American Housewife.'"

00:25:13.120 --> 00:25:16.400
She alleged discriminatory practices by superiors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:16.400 --> 00:25:21.520
and after an internal investigation by ABC,&nbsp;
the creator and producer and a line producer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:21.520 --> 00:25:26.960
were no longer employed by the series. Nearly&nbsp;
two months after the fifth season finale aired,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:26.960 --> 00:25:31.840
it retroactively became a series finale&nbsp;
when ABC canceled "American Housewife."

